[38;5;231m⏺[39m Bounded task — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m lives in [38;5;153mapp.js[39m with one caller, so I'll present a short design in chat rather than write a
  spec. But one thing has to be settled first, because it decides the function's signature.

  [1mThe fork:[0m nothing in this repo currently has a userId. The form collects only username and password ([38;5;153mindex.html:9-10[39m),
  and [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is a stub that never calls [38;5;153mAPI_ENDPOINT[39m ([38;5;153mapp.js:4-8[39m). So "add a userId parameter" needs a source, and the
  three plausible sources give you three different functions.

  - [1mCaller-supplied identifier[0m — [38;5;153mlogin(userId, username, password)[39m. Buys exactly what you asked for; costs a new source
    for the value (a hidden form field, a tenant/account code, something from the page). Until that source exists the
    caller would pass [38;5;153mundefined[39m, so this only pays off if you already know where the value comes from. Signature is
    cheap to change now, expensive once other code calls it.
  - [1mClient-generated correlation ID[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m mints a [38;5;153mcrypto.randomUUID()[39m per attempt and logs it. Buys traceability of
    each login attempt with no new inputs; costs the fact that it identifies the [3mattempt[0m, not the person — it answers
    "which request" rather than "who".
  - [1mServer-returned userId[0m — [38;5;153mlogin()[39m keeps its signature and the userId flows out in the result ([38;5;153m{ success, user, userId
[39m    [38;5;153m}[39m). Buys the real identity of who logged in, which is what "track who logged in" usually means; costs a stub change
    now and a real API response later. This is a return value, not a parameter, so it's not literally what you asked
    for.

  [1mMy recommendation: the third.[0m The username already tells you [3mwho[0m on the way in, so an inbound userId duplicates
  information you have; the thing you don't have is the server's canonical ID for that person. If you have an external
  source for the userId already, though, the first option is the right one and I'll take it.

  One more flag before you pick: [38;5;153mlogin()[39m currently logs the username to the console ([38;5;153mapp.js:5[39m), and whichever option you
  choose I'd be adding an identifier to that log line. That's a browser console in a stub app, but if this is heading
  toward production, identifiers in client-side logs are worth a deliberate decision rather than an inherited one.

